// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THERE-EXISTENTIAL, measured as an APPROXIMATION — «There is a gene.» (core-en
// `ThereExistential` + `ExplNP`). This layer exists to PRICE the approximation, not to ship it.
//
// WHAT CORE-EN DOES, and why it is not this. `there` is `np_expl` — a DISTINCT atomic category with
// a `lex` feature (`there` / `here` / `it`), `indexRel="*NoSem*"`, so it carries no semantics — and
// the existential sits on the VERB: `there-be.np` is `S[dcl] \ np_expl[there] / NP[nom]`. The
// distinct category is the whole point: an expletive cannot fill an ordinary subject slot, and an
// ordinary NP cannot fill the expletive slot. We have no `cat_np_expl` constructor, and adding one
// is a `lexicon:Cat` change (bootstrap ⇒ reseed) plus a `denote_cat` denotation for a category that
// denotes nothing.
//
// THE APPROXIMATION, and why its TRUTH CONDITION is right. `there` is given `something_subj`'s
// category and sem verbatim — type-raised `S/(S\NP)` over `exists_entity_sem` (`λV. ∃x:Entity. V(x)`).
// Composed with the identity copula and an indefinite object it yields
// `∃x. ∃y. gene(y) ∧ x = y`, which is logically equivalent to `∃y. gene(y)`. So this is not a wrong
// meaning; it is a right meaning reached by a route that also licenses wrong SENTENCES.
//
// THE PREDICTED COST, which is what the measurement is for. Categorially `there` becomes
// `something`, so «There affects cells.» should parse as `∃x. affects(x, cells)` — expletive
// `there` outside the existential, which no grammar of English licenses. That is exactly what
// core-en's distinct category rules out, and no guard on top would be a fix rather than a
// Band-Aid: the category is wrong, not the input.
//
// MEASURED 2026-10-27 — REJECTED. NOT merged into `closed-class.esl`.
//
// Battery 78 -> 79 of 89 (the one battery sentence). Page: grammar-gap 466 -> 465, ambiguous
// 427 -> 428, encoded 18 and open 145 UNCHANGED, claim units 63 -> 63. **One page unit, zero
// claim units.**
//
// The over-generation is real and entirely this layer's. On the base NONE of the seven `there`
// sentences parses; with the layer all seven do — the four grammatical ones AND «There affects
// cells.», «There reported genes.», «There is essential.», where the expletive stands as an
// ordinary subject. That is precisely what core-en's distinct `np_expl` rules out, and `match_cat`
// is exact, so there is no subsumption trick that narrows it: the category is wrong, not the input.
// One unit against three ungrammatical forms is not a trade worth making.
//
// WHY THE YIELD IS SO LOW, which is the finding worth keeping. The page carries only FOUR
// existential-`there` units and ALL FOUR are «There is NO ...»:
//   u222 «There is no regional constraint here.»                                    (the 1 win)
//   u349 «There is no middle category here when each tool is scored by its own call.»
//   u509 «There is no second allele for a mutant product to act against.»
//   u637 «There is therefore no deep mutational scan to fall back on.»
// The three that stay gapped fail on their OTHER constructions (a `when`-clause, a `for`-infinitive,
// a fronted `therefore`), not on the existential. So a CORRECT `cat_np_expl` would also have to
// carry the `no`-pivot to be worth anything on this corpus, and even then it tops out near four
// units. The there-existential is a core-en family that this corpus barely exercises — worth
// doing for basic-grammar coverage, not worth a reseed on its own.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

namespace lexicon = "urn:eigenius:lexicon";
namespace core    = "urn:eigenius:core";

resource lexicon:there_expl_subj : lexicon:LexicalEntry {
    core:description = "grammatical: existential `there` — APPROXIMATED as an indefinite subject ('there is a gene').";
    lexicon:form     = "there";
    lexicon:cat      = type_expr( lexicon:fwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:cat_s(lexicon:dcl, lexicon:fin_any), lexicon:bwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:cat_s(lexicon:dcl, lexicon:fin), lexicon:cat_np(lexicon:Entity, lexicon:sg))) );
    lexicon:sem      = lexicon:exists_entity_sem;
    lexicon:sem_type = type_expr( (lexicon:Entity -> Prop) -> Prop );
    lexicon:sense    = "there_expl";
}
resource lexicon:there_expl_subj_pl : lexicon:LexicalEntry {
    core:description = "grammatical: existential `there` with a plural pivot ('there are genes').";
    lexicon:form     = "there";
    lexicon:cat      = type_expr( lexicon:fwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:cat_s(lexicon:dcl, lexicon:fin_any), lexicon:bwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:cat_s(lexicon:dcl, lexicon:fin), lexicon:cat_np(lexicon:Entity, lexicon:pl))) );
    lexicon:sem      = lexicon:exists_entity_sem;
    lexicon:sem_type = type_expr( (lexicon:Entity -> Prop) -> Prop );
    lexicon:sense    = "there_expl";
}
